export const debate = {"id":"dsouza-ehrman-god-suffering-evil-2025","number":"11","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"Dinesh D'Souza vs Bart Ehrman: God, Suffering, and Evil","label":"Problem of evil","date":"2026-08-26","duration":"1 hr 35 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XtWjEjdYvLA","motion":"Can belief in a loving, powerful God survive the scale of suffering and evil in the world?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"D'Souza defended theistic compatibility through epistemic limits, freedom, and natural order; Ehrman pressed suffering's scale, preventability, and unexplained intervention gap.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"Does God have any good reason for allowing the evil and suffering?","context":"D'Souza reframes the alleged contradiction by asking whether divine permission might possess a morally sufficient justification."},"con":{"text":"If God intervenes why doesn't he intervene?","context":"Ehrman connects widespread preventable deaths with the biblical expectation that God answers prayer and sometimes suspends natural regularities."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Theistic answer","speaker":"Dinesh D'Souza","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Skeptical challenge","speaker":"Bart Ehrman","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":79,"con":87},"sections":[{"sectionId":"divine-attributes-and-intervention","title":"Divine Love, Power, and Expected Intervention","timebox":"8:43–54:16","score":{"pro":84,"con":90},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-morally-sufficient-reason-premise","time":"26:17","role":"Major direct reply","words":"The divine attributes conflict with suffering only when supplemented by the premise that God lacks any morally sufficient reason for permitting it.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply accurately exposes the additional premise required for a deductive contradiction and explicitly extends the question from suffering's existence to its immense amount, directly engaging both central skeptical moves. Principal limitation: Establishing that a sufficient reason is possible defeats strict inconsistency but does not establish that the world's actual suffering is expected, probable, or well explained under theism. Live burden: The theistic case must supply independently plausible candidate reasons and show their proportional relationship to severity, distribution, innocent victims, natural harms, and apparently selective divine rescue. Locked score: The very strong band reflects excellent diagnostic precision, calibrated modal force, and decisive motion contact, while preserving the important boundary between blocking a logical contradiction and answering the stronger evidential challenge.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-divine-triad-tension","time":"8:43","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Divine love, divine power, and existing suffering jointly create the traditional apparent contradiction at the center of the problem of evil.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move identifies the governing philosophical tension with unusually direct burden contact, carefully presenting an apparent contradiction rather than prematurely claiming a formally demonstrated inconsistency among the three propositions. Principal limitation: This opening formulation does not yet articulate the indispensable bridging premise that perfect love and power would exclude every observed instance, leaving morally sufficient permission available as a conceptual response. Live burden: The skeptical case must convert this clean prima facie tension into an evidential argument sensitive to suffering's quantity, distribution, apparent pointlessness, and the theist's proposed reasons for restraint. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional relevance, disciplined qualification, and coherent issue framing, while recognizing that the excerpt establishes the central problem more successfully than its ultimate resolution.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-selective-miracles-stable-laws","time":"51:24","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Predictable natural regularities enable consequential freedom, while biblical miracles are selective events serving spiritual purposes rather than routine suspensions of causation.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply supplies a substantial reason for divine restraint by connecting predictable causal regularity with deliberation, learning, responsibility, and consequences, while distinguishing exceptional miracles from constant disruption. Principal limitation: It does not identify principled criteria governing miraculous selection or show why rare prevention of catastrophic harm would destabilize ordinary expectations, education, or morally significant agency. Live burden: The theistic case must explain why biblical interventions establish no comparable expectation today and why a stable world cannot include limited rescue, warning, mitigation, or altered victim vulnerability. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct engagement with the intervention discrepancy and a coherent regularity argument, moderated by overstated necessity, underspecified miracle purposes, incomplete comparison with alternative laws, and failure to resolve suffering's scale and selectivity.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-preventable-suffering-intervention-gap","time":"18:51","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Massive preventable suffering conflicts evidentially with a biblical God portrayed as answering prayer, suspending natural laws, and intervening for people in need.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move combines concrete scale claims with a precisely targeted expectation of answered prayer and biblical intervention, producing a powerful discrepancy rather than relying on suffering's emotional vividness alone. Principal limitation: The numerical examples are not substantiated within the excerpt, and the argument does not specify what frequency or pattern of rescue an interventionist theology actually predicts under its strongest qualified form. Live burden: The skeptical case must defend those empirical premises and explain why selective providence, unknown reasons, stable laws, or human responsibility cannot plausibly account for the observed scarcity of protection. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional motion-level relevance, clear logical structure, and charitable focus on an interventionist conception, with limited reservation for evidentiary sourcing and an incompletely formalized expectation.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"epistemic-limits-and-scriptural-explanations","title":"Epistemic Limits and Scriptural Explanations","timebox":"12:25–31:26","score":{"pro":85,"con":79},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-job-limited-human-knowledge","time":"28:17","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Job suggests that limited human perspective prevents an inference from our inability to discern God's reason to the conclusion that no reason exists.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply develops Job's divine speech into a clear epistemic principle, carefully distinguishing limited human perspective from divine knowledge and avoiding any claim to know the hidden reason itself. Principal limitation: Skeptical humility blocks a confident inference from unseen reasons to nonexistent reasons, yet by itself supplies neither positive explanatory content nor a probability assessment concerning suffering's observed scale and distribution. Live burden: The theistic case must explain when cognitive limitation legitimately restrains inference without indiscriminately insulating every possible divine-permission claim from ordinary evidential evaluation. Locked score: The very strong band reflects disciplined qualification, strong logical coherence, and an effective answer to the denial-only interpretation of Job, tempered because the empirical intervention discrepancy and particular cases of suffering remain substantially unanswered.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-punishment-account-innocence","time":"12:25","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Scriptural punishment accounts connect suffering with sin and repentance, yet innocent sufferers remain difficult to reconcile with that recurring explanation.","score":81,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move isolates a genuine pressure on punishment-centered theodicies by contrasting their sin-to-suffering structure with innocent victims, while charitably allowing that the account need not represent scripture's exclusive explanation. Principal limitation: Its expansive characterization of recurrence across both testaments is asserted rather than textually demonstrated, and collective covenantal punishment may operate differently from claims about each individual's personal desert. Live burden: The challenge must distinguish corporate, disciplinary, and individual accounts, then show why even their strongest qualified versions cannot morally explain innocents caught within the resulting suffering. Locked score: The strong band reflects clear central-burden contact, coherent reasoning, and a plausible scriptural pattern, moderated by insufficient documentation of breadth, exceptions, and the precise unit of responsibility.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-job-denial-not-explanation","time":"15:10","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Job's divine speeches appear to deny the innocent sufferer's standing to question God instead of supplying an intelligible reason for his suffering.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move draws an analytically valuable distinction between explaining suffering and denying a sufferer's authority to demand an explanation, grounding that distinction in the divine challenge addressed to Job. Principal limitation: The characterization compresses Job's complex literary architecture and underrepresents the plausible epistemic reading on which the speeches disclose creaturely limitation rather than merely humiliating or silencing the questioner. Live burden: The skeptical interpretation must show why an epistemic lesson, cosmic-order perspective, or transformed relationship still fails to constitute the relevant kind of explanation for Job's particular innocent suffering. Locked score: The strong band recognizes coherent interpretive pressure and meaningful scriptural-plurality contact, while reserving substantial credit because the excerpt underdocuments its broader claim about what most of Job provides.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"freedom-and-moral-evil","title":"Human Freedom and Moral Evil","timebox":"31:35–83:02","score":{"pro":74,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-freedom-permits-moral-evil","time":"31:35","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Human beings cause moral atrocities through misused freedom, and categorical divine prevention would threaten the consequential agency integral to genuine moral choice.","score":80,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply gives moral evil a recognizable causal and moral structure by locating atrocities in human agency, then connecting significant choice with consequences rather than assigning perpetration directly to God. Principal limitation: It moves too quickly from rejecting categorical prevention to permitting actual extremes, without showing that occasional rescue, constrained consequences, or prevention of catastrophic outcomes would eliminate meaningful freedom. Live burden: The defense must establish a principled threshold for divine restraint and explain why victims bear devastating consequences when perpetrators could retain morally significant intentions and choices under less destructive conditions. Locked score: The strong band reflects direct central-burden contact and an intelligible freedom-to-consequence bridge, moderated by incomplete treatment of selective intervention, severity, natural suffering, and the distinction between choosing evil and successfully inflicting it.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-human-agency-does-not-remove-intervention-burden","time":"44:27","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Human perpetrators may cause atrocities, but their responsibility does not answer why the interventionist Christian God permits victims to suffer without rescue.","score":88,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply cleanly separates perpetrator responsibility from a capable third party's decision not to rescue, thereby preserving the freedom defense while exposing the distinct intervention question it leaves unresolved. Principal limitation: Its phrase about the Christian viewpoint generalizes across diverse doctrines, and the brief excerpt offers neither representative biblical cases nor a defended prediction about contemporary intervention frequency. Live burden: The skeptical case must establish which scriptural commitments generate an expectation of rescue and why selective providence, eschatological compensation, or freedom-preserving restraint cannot satisfy those commitments. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional responsiveness, logical clarity, central-burden contact, and charitable preservation of human causation, with modest limitation for doctrinal breadth and insufficiently developed evidence concerning the expected pattern of intervention.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-fall-autonomy-consequential-freedom","time":"80:53","role":"Supporting reply","words":"The Fall narrative connects humanity's chosen autonomy with a lawful world where freedom operates through stable and consequential actions.","score":66,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply integrates autonomy, stable regularity, and consequential freedom into a coherent theological narrative, giving the Fall a specific explanatory function rather than invoking it as an undefined source of disorder. Principal limitation: It shifts from Adam and Eve to collective humanity without defending inherited responsibility, and it asserts that this kind of world is required without demonstrating proportionality or modal necessity. Live burden: The account must explain why unchosen victims inherit exposure to natural hazards and others' atrocities, why humanity's supposed counterfactual choice is credible, and why safer consequences would undermine autonomy. Locked score: The mixed band reflects a recognizable subsidiary bridge and useful contact with lawfulness and restraint, tempered by unsupported collective scope, theological presupposition, overstatement, and failure to explain the actual distribution of preventable suffering.","tags":[]},"con":null}]},{"sectionId":"natural-law-and-life-supporting-conditions","title":"Natural Law, Fine-Tuning, and Natural Suffering","timebox":"35:13–48:20","score":{"pro":75,"con":92},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-tectonics-supports-habitable-earth","time":"35:13","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Earthquake-producing plate tectonics also supports biodiversity, climatic stability, and other conditions important to Earth's long-term habitability.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move replaces an abstract appeal to natural law with a concrete benefit-cost example, connecting earthquake-producing tectonics to biodiversity, temperature regulation, and protection relevant to terrestrial habitability. Principal limitation: The cited benefits support importance within Earth's actual system but do not establish modal necessity, especially for every conceivable life-supporting arrangement available to an omnipotent creator. Live burden: The argument must document its scientific claims and show why comparable goods could not arise through safer tectonic rates, different planetary mechanisms, altered vulnerabilities, or selective mitigation of catastrophic effects. Locked score: The strong band reflects substantial relevance and a plausible empirical connection between hazardous processes and sustaining goods, tempered by overstated necessity, indirect scholarly invocation, and failure to compare realistic or divinely possible alternatives.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-actual-laws-not-necessary-laws","time":"45:06","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Describing life's dependence on existing laws and tectonics does not prove that an omnipotent creator could not sustain life under safer alternative conditions.","score":92,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely identifies the modal gap between describing conditions necessary under the actual system and proving those conditions necessary across the creative options attributed to an omnipotent God. Principal limitation: Showing that alternatives remain conceptually open does not positively demonstrate that a safer law-governed world containing comparable embodied agents is metaphysically, logically, or physically coherent. Live burden: The skeptical case must develop plausible alternative arrangements or explain why the theist, having asserted necessity, bears the decisive evidential burden without demanding a complete counter-cosmology. Locked score: The very strong band reflects outstanding diagnostic accuracy, direct engagement with both tectonics and fine-tuning, careful possibility-level calibration, and charitable treatment of conditional necessity, while acknowledging that the objection defeats an inference rather than constructing an alternative universe.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-lawful-fine-tuned-human-world","time":"37:48","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"A stable universe containing human beings depends on finely calibrated physical conditions whose alteration under the actual framework could eliminate life or the universe.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move connects the lawful-creation defense to modern fine-tuning considerations and clearly explains why predictable, calibrated conditions matter for a universe capable of containing specifically human embodied life. Principal limitation: It shifts from sensitivity under actual laws to the stronger conclusion that this arrangement is the available way to produce life, without comparing alternative laws or divine creative possibilities. Live burden: The defense must specify which parameters are varied, justify the relevant probability framework, and connect cosmic calibration to the necessity or permissibility of local biological suffering and natural disasters. Locked score: The mixed band reflects strong central relevance and a recoverable conditional argument, but also substantial modal overstatement, ambiguous parameter scope, limited evidentiary development, and incomplete contact with the challenge concerning safer lawful creations.","tags":[]},"con":null}]},{"sectionId":"meaning-compassion-and-practical-response","title":"Meaning, Compassion, and Responses to Suffering","timebox":"66:55–94:15","score":{"pro":70,"con":78},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-christian-universalized-moral-concern","time":"73:45","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Christianity historically widened moral obligation beyond immediate communities and helped shape Western revulsion toward distant suffering, including among nonbelievers.","score":64,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply introduces a historically interesting genealogy of universal concern and directly contests the assumption that contemporary secular moral intuitions developed independently of Christianity's widening account of obligation. Principal limitation: It generalizes across cultures, Western atheists, and long historical periods, then advances an unsupported counterfactual that broad revulsion would not exist without Christianity despite numerous alternative influences. Live burden: The argument must offer comparative historical evidence, define the moral attitudes being traced, and distinguish Christianity's possible contribution from claims of necessity, uniqueness, or present justificatory dependence. Locked score: The weak band reflects meaningful subsidiary relevance and partial contact with secular independence, substantially outweighed by unstable scope, inadequate comparative warrant, counterfactual overreach, and failure to address whether historical influence establishes theological truth.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-consolation-not-explanatory-truth","time":"66:55","role":"Diagnostic challenge","words":"Religious consolation may benefit sufferers, but emotional solace neither establishes the belief's truth nor supplies an intellectually adequate explanation for suffering.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply draws a conceptually exact boundary between psychological consolation, propositional truth, and explanatory adequacy, preventing practical comfort from being treated as evidence for the worldview that provides it. Principal limitation: Although the distinction is sound, the selected move does not analyze the actual freedom, stable-law, epistemic-limit, or sufficient-reason premises advanced by the opposing side. Live burden: The skeptical case must apply this truth-versus-benefit distinction to those concrete warrants and demonstrate their explanatory failure rather than merely observing that comfort alone cannot verify them. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent coherence, warrant, precision, and charitable acknowledgment of solace, tempered by limited responsiveness to the decisive theodicy components and by its subsidiary rather than motion-completing contribution.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-christian-compassion-and-service","time":"90:01","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Christian discipleship calls believers to imitate Christ through compassionate service and permits cooperation with atheists in practical efforts to reduce suffering.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply ends with a concrete ethic of imitation, service, and cooperation, fairly recognizing that atheists can participate in relieving suffering while explaining how Christian belief may motivate believers. Principal limitation: Claims that Christianity distinctively expands compassion or that secular respect is fraying remain broadly scoped and weakly supported, while practical fruit does not establish explanatory truth or answer divine nonintervention. Live burden: The argument must distinguish one powerful motivation from a uniquely necessary foundation and connect human service to the debate's separate question about why a loving, powerful God permits suffering. Locked score: The strong band reflects coherent practical relevance, charitable inclusion, and a plausible motivational account, moderated by limited evidence for cultural distinctiveness and incomplete responsiveness to the motion-level explanatory and intervention burdens.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-secular-concern-and-finite-suffering","time":"70:11","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Shared humanity supports concern for suffering without belief in God, while evolutionary finitude offers a natural explanation for vulnerability and hardship.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move persuasively rejects the claim that present moral concern requires theistic belief, grounding compassion in shared humanity while offering evolutionary finitude as an available naturalistic context for suffering. Principal limitation: The evolutionary explanation remains briefly sketched, and establishing secular concern neither settles its historical genealogy nor directly answers the theistic arguments from stable laws and possible sufficient reasons. Live burden: The skeptical case must distinguish the justification of compassion from its cultural origins, then explain how naturalistic vulnerability accounts for suffering without claiming that causal explanation alone resolves every normative concern. Locked score: The mixed band reflects coherent, charitable, and relevant support for a secular practical response, offset by limited engagement with central opposing premises and underdeveloped explanatory detail.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":79,"strengths":["Precisely identified the hidden no-sufficient-reason premise required to transform the divine-attributes tension into a deductive contradiction.","Developed Job's speeches into a disciplined epistemic-limits argument without pretending to know God's particular reasons for permitting suffering.","Connected moral freedom and predictable natural regularity to consequential agency, supplying recognizable reasons for restraint rather than bare mystery.","Concluded with a concrete ethic of Christian service and explicitly welcomed cooperation with atheists in relieving suffering."],"blunders":[{"text":"The natural-law case repeatedly moved from benefits within the actual world to necessity across every possible divine arrangement without establishing that modal bridge.","links":[]},{"text":"The freedom defense did not explain why selective prevention of catastrophic consequences would destroy meaningful choice or ordinary causal predictability.","links":[]},{"text":"The historical claim that widespread moral concern depends counterfactually on Christianity exceeded the comparative evidence and left alternative genealogies insufficiently considered.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":87,"strengths":["Made suffering's scale and apparent preventability central to a focused discrepancy between biblical intervention and present nonrescue.","Preserved human responsibility for atrocities while clearly separating it from the unresolved question of divine intervention on behalf of victims.","Diagnosed the gap between actual laws and necessary laws, directly answering both the tectonics and fine-tuning defenses.","Distinguished consolation from truth and defended secular compassion without denying that religious traditions may have historical influence."],"blunders":[{"text":"The scriptural punishment critique characterized a broad recurring pattern without documenting its scope, exceptions, or collective covenantal setting in sufficient detail.","links":[]},{"text":"The interpretation of Job compressed a complex literary work and underdeveloped the competing view that the divine speeches convey an epistemic lesson.","links":[]},{"text":"The secular-response material was relevant but did not directly test the strongest premises behind the sufficient-reason and stable-law defenses.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that suffering can remain compatible with a loving and powerful God when freedom, stable agency, epistemic limits, and proportionate divine purposes jointly provide a cumulative permission account.","premises":["A morally significant world requires agents whose deliberations normally produce stable consequences, because choices without dependable effects cannot sustain responsibility, learning, trust, planning, or durable interpersonal commitments.","Stable agency does not require absolute nonintervention, but selective intervention may reasonably remain limited when its regular availability would redirect moral attention from responsibility and prevention toward expectations of supernatural rescue.","Observed natural processes can produce both sustaining goods and grave hazards, so their moral assessment should consider interconnected systemic benefits while avoiding the unsupported claim that the actual physical arrangement is uniquely necessary.","Human cognitive limits justify modesty about declaring every unexplained suffering pointless, although that modesty must accompany positive candidate reasons and cannot substitute for engagement with apparently excessive or preventable harms.","A credible theistic response should treat compassionate prevention, relief, and solidarity as expected human obligations, making practical resistance to suffering part of the proposed purpose rather than evidence that suffering is intrinsically good."],"conclusion":"Taken cumulatively, these considerations permit a coherent and morally serious theistic interpretation of suffering, though they establish compatibility and practical responsibility rather than a complete explanation of every instance."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Expectation-shaping restraint","text":"If protective intervention were sufficiently regular and publicly predictable, institutions and individuals could rationally externalize prevention, insurance, rescue, and accountability to divine action. The relevant good is therefore not mere physical regularity but a moral environment in which agents reasonably expect their choices and omissions to matter. This does not justify every nonintervention; it supplies a narrower reason why intervention might remain exceptional while preserving human duties to reduce foreseeable harm."},{"title":"Cumulative rather than exclusive goods","text":"Freedom, natural regularity, epistemic distance, and opportunities for solidarity need not explain suffering independently or exhaustively. Their force can instead be cumulative: different goods may constrain different categories of divine action, while no single consideration bears the entire justificatory load. This structure answers the objection that freedom says little about earthquakes or that tectonics says little about atrocities, although the combined case still owes a proportionality account for extreme and apparently gratuitous harms."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that the scale, distribution, and apparent preventability of suffering are substantially less expected under a loving, powerful, interventionist God than under hypotheses lacking such governance.","premises":["A loving and powerful interventionist God creates a reasonable expectation of at least some detectable relationship between need, innocence, prayer, preventability, and rescue, even if universal protection is not promised.","Freedom explains why perpetrators can choose wrongdoing, but it does not by itself explain why victims must experience every intended consequence or why limited rescue would erase the perpetrator's morally significant choice.","Benefits supplied by actual natural systems establish conditional tradeoffs, not that an omnipotent creator lacked safer physical, biological, or providential means of securing comparable goods for embodied agents.","Epistemic humility should reduce confidence in both directions: unknown purposes remain possible, but unknown purposes cannot receive decisive evidential weight without independent support or principled limits on their use.","Compassionate practices and consoling beliefs can possess genuine human value while remaining evidentially independent from the truth of claims about divine governance and the explanation of suffering."],"conclusion":"The cumulative discrepancy therefore supplies substantial evidence against the interventionist divine model under discussion, while stopping short of proving that no deity or morally sufficient reason is possible."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Distributional signature test","text":"Competing worldviews can be compared through the distributional signatures they make reasonable to expect. An interventionist model need not predict universal safety, but it should arguably predict some relationship between rescue and morally relevant variables such as innocence, urgency, sincere prayer, or preventability. If outcomes instead track ordinary causal access, wealth, geography, and medical capacity without a detectable additional pattern, that absence becomes evidentially relevant while remaining compatible with occasional unexplained recoveries."},{"title":"Minimum-disruption rescue","text":"Many conceivable interventions would require little disruption of freedom or stable law: a warning arriving in time, a weapon malfunctioning, a pathogen failing to transmit, or a rescuer noticing danger. Because such events resemble ordinary contingencies, occasional protective outcomes need not make agency unintelligible or natural regularities unreliable. The challenge is therefore not why God fails to suspend causation constantly, but why minimally disruptive rescue appears absent across so many extreme cases where the costs to victims are irreversible."}]}},"year":2025};
